1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,464
So, welcome to your introductory geography lecture.

2
00:00:03,464 --> 00:00:05,720
We'll begin with some basics.

3
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:09,520
Firstly, what do we learn by studying geography?

4
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:14,510
Well, we learn a great deal about all the processes that have affected

5
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:17,580
and that continue to affect the earth's surface.

6
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:20,312
But we learn far more than that,

7
00:00:20,312 --> 00:00:27,338
because studying geography also informs us about the different kinds of relationships that develop between a particular environment

8
00:00:27,338 --> 00:00:29,680
and the people that live there.

9
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:29,730
Okay.

10
00:00:29,730 --> 00:00:35,040
We like to think of geography as having two main branches.

11
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:40,493
There's the study of the nature of our planet - its physical features, what it actually looks like -

12
00:00:40,493 --> 00:00:44,983
and then there's the study of the ways in which we choose to live

13
00:00:44,983 --> 00:00:47,870
and of the impact of those on our planet.

14
00:00:47,870 --> 00:00:51,460
Our current use of carbon fuels is a good example of that.

15
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:54,882
But there are more specific study areas to consider too,

16
00:00:54,882 --> 00:00:59,330
and we'll be looking at each of these in turn throughout this semester.

17
00:00:59,330 --> 00:01:05,232
These include bio-physical geography, by which I mean the study of the natural environment

18
00:01:05,232 --> 00:01:07,340
and all its living things.

19
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:12,750
Then there's topography- that looks at the shapes of the land and oceans.

20
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:17,400
There's the study of political geography and social geography too, of course,

21
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:20,500
which is the study of communities of people.

22
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:25,987
We have economic geography - in which we examine all kinds of resources

23
00:01:25,987 --> 00:01:28,730
and their use -agriculture, for example.

24
00:01:28,730 --> 00:01:34,195
Next comes historical geography - the understanding of how people and their environments

25
00:01:34,195 --> 00:01:39,660
and the ways they interact have changed over a period of time -

26
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:43,675
and urban geography, an aspect I'm particularly interested in,

27
00:01:43,675 --> 00:01:49,919
which takes as its focus the location of cities, the services that those cities

28
00:01:49,919 --> 00:01:50,365
provide,

29
00:01:50,365 --> 00:01:54,380
and migration of people to and from such cities.

30
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:57,540
And lastly, we have cartography.

31
00:01:57,540 --> 00:01:59,985
That's the art and science of map-making.

32
00:01:59,985 --> 00:02:02,430
You'll be doing a lot of that!

33
00:02:02,430 --> 00:02:08,860
So, to summarise before we continue, we now have our key answer..,

34
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:15,907
studying this subject is important because without geographical knowledge, we would know very little about our surroundings

35
00:02:15,907 --> 00:02:21,710
and we wouldn't be able to identify all the problems that relate to them.

36
00:02:21,710 --> 00:02:26,455
So, by definition, we wouldn't be in an informed position to work out how

37
00:02:26,455 --> 00:02:28,150
to solve any of them.

38
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:31,390
Okay, now for some practicalities.

39
00:02:31,390 --> 00:02:34,630
What do geographers actually do?

40
00:02:34,630 --> 00:02:37,610
Well, we collect data to begin with!

41
00:02:37,610 --> 00:02:40,550
You'll be doing a lot of that on your first field trip!

42
00:02:40,550 --> 00:02:42,467
How do we do this?

43
00:02:42,467 --> 00:02:44,000
There are several means.

44
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,486
We might, for example, conduct a census - count a population in a given

45
00:02:49,486 --> 00:02:50,330
area perhaps.

46
00:02:50,330 --> 00:02:57,180
We also need images of the earth's surface which we can produce by means of computer-generation technology

47
00:02:57,180 --> 00:03:00,000
or with the help of satellite relays.

48
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,878
We've come a very long way from the early exploration of the world by

49
00:03:04,878 --> 00:03:09,060
sailing ships when geographers only had pens and paper at their disposal.

50
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:14,300
After we've gathered our information, we must analyse it!

51
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:20,110
We need to look for patterns, most commonly those of causes and consequences.

52
00:03:20,110 --> 00:03:24,759
This kind of information helps us to predict and resolve problems that could affect

53
00:03:24,759 --> 00:03:26,420
the world we live in.

54
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:30,200
But we don't keep all this information confidential.

55
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:34,724
We then need to publish our findings so that other people can access it

56
00:03:34,724 --> 00:03:36,340
and be informed by it.

57
00:03:36,340 --> 00:03:40,908
And one way in which this information can be published is in the form

58
00:03:40,908 --> 00:03:41,560
of maps.

59
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:46,000
You'll all have used one at some stage of your life already.

60
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,310
Let's consider the benefits of maps from a geographer's perspective.

61
00:03:50,310 --> 00:03:55,113
Maps can be folded and put in a pocket and can provide a great

62
00:03:55,113 --> 00:03:58,200
store of reference when they're collected into an atlas.

63
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:02,546
They can depict the physical features of the entire planet if necessary,

64
00:04:02,546 --> 00:04:06,530
or, just a small part of it in much greater detail.

65
00:04:06,530 --> 00:04:08,320
But there is a drawback.

66
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:13,716
You can't exactly replicate something that is three-dimensional, like our planet, on a flat

67
00:04:13,716 --> 00:04:14,872
piece of paper,

68
00:04:14,872 --> 00:04:17,184
because paper has only two dimensions,

69
00:04:17,184 --> 00:04:22,580
and that means there'll always be a certain degree of distortion on a map.

70
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:23,910
It can't be avoided.

71
00:04:23,910 --> 00:04:30,761
We can also use aerial photographs.., pictures taken by cameras at high altitude above

72
00:04:30,761 --> 00:04:31,740
the earth.

73
00:04:31,740 --> 00:04:36,507
These are great for showing all kinds of geographical features that are not easy

74
00:04:36,507 --> 00:04:38,210
to see from the ground.

75
00:04:38,210 --> 00:04:43,440
You can easily illustrate areas of diseased trees or how much traffic is on

76
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:48,670
the roads at a given time or information about deep sea beds, for example.

77
00:04:48,670 --> 00:04:50,678
Then there are Landsats.

78
00:04:50,678 --> 00:04:57,706
These are satellites that circle the earth and transmit visual information to computers at

79
00:04:57,706 --> 00:04:58,710
receiving stations.

80
00:04:58,710 --> 00:05:03,377
They circle the earth several times a day and can provide a mass of

81
00:05:03,377 --> 00:05:07,710
information - you'll all be familiar with the information they give us about the

82
00:05:07,710 --> 00:05:08,710
weather, for example.

83
00:05:08,710 --> 00:05:13,218
So, what we're going to do now is look at a short presentation in

84
00:05:13,218 --> 00:05:15,150
which you'll see all these tools...
